<h2>INTRODUCTION: FLIGHT OF THE EAGLE AND DEEP FALL</h2>
<h3>(ON THE DEATH OF G. DELEUZE)</h3>
<p>Philosophers have always had a specific relationship with death, since it allows them to die well within the banality of the everyday in order to be able to detect the flaw or fracture where thought is susceptible to being inscribed. When it is found that the philosopher in question has thought about this before living his life, one can suppose that life and death have interacted, in the work and in experience, in a somewhat unusual way. And indeed, with a surprising cheerfulness, Deleuze, at the beginning of his "abecedary," evoking the "clause" [closing] of a broadcast, speaks of his own death. A Claire Parnet, giggling, with some of those laughter held back in the cavern of her sonorous thorax, which inevitably brings to mind this formula from Kafka cited by him: "My teeth give rendezvous to my lungs behind my back," he said: "To speak of things on which I have not reflected first, is inconceivable. What saves me a little is the clause: that will not be used until after my death. So, you understand, I already feel myself reduced to the state of a pure archive of Pierre-André Boutang, as a sheet of paper, and almost to the state of pure spirit. I speak after my death, and if I have sufficiently turned tables so that it is clear that a spirit, it is not just anyone who gives very intelligent answers, it is always somewhat summary. So, we go on, I begin, A, B, C, D, whatever you want."</p>

<p>The reason for this cheerfulness? If real death is ultimately nothing compared to the many times the work will have "crossed Acheron" (Phie, p. 190), where the philosopher will have had to endure "with his air of returning from the land of the dead" (p. 67, G. DELEUZE, with F. GUATTARI, What is Philosophy?, Minuit, Paris, 1991, cited Phie.), perhaps it is because this Hades brought back life, a more intense life, "more beautiful qualities, brighter colors, brilliant stones, precious stones, more vibrant extensions (DR, p. 314 G. DELEUZE, Difference and Repetition, PUF, Paris, 1985, cited DR.), in short a life capable of overcoming empirical death.</p>

<p>The first page of What is Philosophy? evoked several great deaths in surgery (Monet, Titian, Turner, Kant, Chateaubriand), who, in their advanced old age, found the resource to launch themselves again, to reinvent their work, hence their boldness. Deleuze will derive from this, further in the book, a distinction between two ways of aging: aging according to History, and becoming-old—to the term "becoming" we must grant all the literary weight it takes on in Deleuzian thought: "On one hand, one ages while following History, and with it, on the other hand, one experiences old age through a very discreet event (perhaps the same event that allows the question to be posed 'what is philosophy?'). And it is the same thing for those who die young, and there are several ways of dying thus" (p. 106)—to know how to die young according to History or becoming-young-dead.</p>

<p>This distinction revolves around which is not an entirely determined empirical matter: one can be a young old person, that is to say, still young, yet carrying the weight of age; one can also die young without there being any real difference between that death and that of an old person, and vice versa: one can, while being entirely burdened with age, become a young dead person or let the discreet event of becoming pass through oneself, making old age an absolute renewal. The true difference lies between a partial subjugation to History, to the state of things, to the implacable necessity of the course of the world (young soldiers mown down by wars, adolescents or young family girls who die on weekend trips, and are enrolled in the statistics emitted by a society at such a stage of consumerist development, which includes this type of leisure, this type of transport, etc.), and on the other hand a way to seize the occasion or the event for one to play again in some way on the spot, replay the trajectory, reinvent its rules, open an alternative, make a detour, bifurcate the trajectory; so the young person who becomes a young dead person in History: he may be, in the most direct way, a direct revolutionary who dies against a barricade to promote a better world, but also, conversely, as Rimbaud did at twenty, who arrives at a kind of literary suicide, for having noticed that poetry was not the revolution he believed it to be, as she was incapable of "changing life" and restoring man to his primitive and auroral state of "son of the sun," and for which then was the way to launch himself again, to die from life to live, in the occurrence of surviving by living off the trade of death, trafficking arms in Africa—rather than a solution of ease, of continuing to write, according to the inert perseverance of that which would have caused this creation to cease in order to be nothing more than its competence. But to die while living, the destiny of Rimbaud, can be a paradoxical way of starting over or taking back the initiative even when one has failed, and to become by means of a young death, or we can also live while dying: and we find ourselves on the side of those "old people" who make their old age and the shadow of their death not a sudden decrepitude, but the occasion for a new birth, of a life otherwise relaunched or of a becoming-young—think of the old Kant, writing the Critique of Judgment, and thus transgressing all the limits he had passed to establish, iconoclast of his own thought, fostering as well as the influence of Kantianism, that is to say, German idealism. Similarly Chateaubriand discovers in his own old age the possibility of becoming a source for the way to turn toward the future and the modernity of literature as a "mysterious point," according to the words of Julien Gracq.</p>

<p>Similarly, Deleuze, though he did not claim for himself such a revolutionary relationship to oneself ("We cannot claim such a status," Phie, p. 8), and the very discreet event that takes place at this delicate balance, rare and miraculous point of a certain old age, as this light illuminates contours all the more sharp that they prefigure the nocturnal engulfment, is the simple question, which every philosopher, it seems to him, must ask: "what is philosophy?" Now, the question is not banal, but it is revolutionary because there they break the gears of evident practices, the course of a life that could not question things in that way, precisely because it consisted in practicing philosophy, and to occult, through the innocence of business, by the urgency of realization, the question of what discipline it is.</p>

<p>Thus even more literally and more dramatically, and in a body to body more tightly pressed of life and death, of youth and old age, the suicide of Deleuze. A long-awaited, announced, dreaded death, since it is the term of a long-term illness. And yet, refusing to be its subject, and reclaiming the initiative, he had to make a choice of living, a becoming that has all the force of emergence and intemporality of life. "And two or three years ago, in his afterword to Quad by Beckett, Gilles Deleuze evoked the figure of Beckett at the end of his life, Beckett 'the exhausted': 'The exhausted, it is much more than the fatigued [...] The fatigued only experiences realization, while the exhausted exhausts all possibility [...] Does he exhaust the possible since he is himself an exhausted man, or is he exhausted because he has exhausted the possible? [...]'." Reading further—and one cannot help but think of Deleuze himself, exhausted by his serious respiratory problems and increasingly unable to move—, he added: "It is the most horrible position to wait for death, sitting without being able to neither cry nor laugh, watching the blow that will make us stand up for one last time and lay us down forever" ("The gesture of a philosopher," Libération, November 6, 1995, p. 8). Deleuze was not just fatigued, fatigued by the accomplishment of such a project, a fatigue to which rest would have provided the solution, a fatigue susceptible to recharge and relaunch toward a new possible, but he was exhausted, without any more possibility of horizon, like Beckett in his wheelchair in his asylum-like old age. But Beckett the exhausted man—could he not even be able to invent a final possibility based on the total impossibility of total exhaustion: "he had to live," by this exhausted means, and in the most radical way that is, through his larval and impotent characters that adorn his work, he embodied it, rather he played it otherwise, in his body, as he had already played it through his writing. Deleuze the exhausted, the event of the awaited death—even a non-event also, or the event inscribed in the course of things—Derrida writes, "the infinite of another event" ("It will be necessary to erase everything," Libération, November 7, 1995, p. 37): his manner of dying, is thus a decision, invention and affirmative affirmation, like flight, where the mortifying crushing or the negative is not a secondary consequence, but rather aimed at as such—according to which his work will have had no reason to attribute anything to the negative. This always appears as a consequence of an abundant surplus of positivity; this is the sense of "ethics of intensive magnitudes" which is the question in Difference and Repetition and where the principle that is "zero," the discharge of maximum intensity or of 1, is itself invested with positivity by this maximum that is exhaustion: "It takes the power of a Cascade or of a deep fall to make degradation a movement toward affirmation. This is the flight of the eagle, which first overlooks, suspends and descends. You go as far down as possible, and by that movement you affirm the lowest point—asymmetric synthesis" (DR, p. 302).</p>

<p>That the negative never lasts forever, that it can no longer be anything other than a condition of an affirmation, is also the sense of the Nietzschean reading of the struggle between master and slave: it is the superiority of the master that he is nothing but affirmation, negation being the product of inferior beings (the slaves) as it is only a derived aspect of this affirmative power, but not lacking the fact that he is [the master] since I am good while you are evil); the inferiority of the slave consisting, conversely, in that his only "yes" which he must be capable of is a pale or recriminating derivative of "no": "you: you are evil, master oppressor, you are not good, but I, the slave, and I, I am not that—therefore I am good." It is the thought of flaws, where the flaw is not born except from slow accumulation of resentment and grievances (cf. NP, p. 136 G. DELEUZE, Nietzsche and Philosophy, PUF, Paris, 1976, cited NP.). If the slave is here, the slave must also be affirmed—as a final form of distancing from oneself toward affirmation, but still an affirmative affirmation: the affirmation of extinction for one more relaunch, a very real return of the affirmation, or counter-finality to interiorize any finalizing project: as Leibniz did, who does not let the cause bring the consequence to its end by the simple fact that he is the one who follows it.</p>

<p>Similarly for suicide: to give it its maximum Deleuzian character, is to make an affirmation of it, the affirmation of a singular event, a way of dying, where the essential is not death, but the invention of an unprecedented manner: "Too much to say about what happened to us there, with a dread we knew without doubt (we knew he was sick), but also with this certain death, as an unimaginable image, which, during the event, would have hollowed out, as it were, the infinite painfulness of another event" (J. Derrida). Even so "the painfulnesses" are they at the height of the affirmation of the event, and to be Deleuzian until the end, would it not be possible to neutralize the impact of these affective and passive affects? More Deleuzianly, one can find this in the sense of the article by Lortatou, at the same page of this number of Libération: "In this end of his life there was no nihilism, it was the affirmation. Even in the middle of the illness and death. Why did I talk to you about the past? He laughed, he was there. It is your idiot jargon, it says." Grief, and death, are two things; only the purely affirmative moment of flight remains, the recreation of a possible aerial flight even when everything is blocked by the exhausted; and it is precisely this that is missing—how not to hear in this an echo of this formula from Kierkegaard cited by Deleuze: "Of the possible, of the possible, one suffocates" or, other expression of James, who "claims the oxygen of possibility" (LS, p. 370 G. DELEUZE, Logic of Sense, Minuit, Paris, 1969, cited LS.)?</p>

<p>Does not this last way of doing things represent, once more, that "life [is] laughter and action" (NP, p. 212), by which Deleuze, whatever his age of body and mind is, becomes a young dead person who has lived—as if he had seen his History in passing and as if it implied, eventually, his death. This is the antipode of the thesis supported by Didier Eribon in The New Observer, and which he symptomatically titled "The last 'no' of Deleuze"—I would prefer here the opposite, the last "yes": "The physical fatigue, the terrible fragility of health that overwhelmed Gilles Deleuze for so long will have then been the reason for his courage and his resistance... He was afraid of being trapped, and it is true that there is something tragically unbearable in this 'death' "voluntary of a philosopher who loved to exalt the forces of affirmation and always called, in the last instance, upon the resources of life" (Le Nouvel Observateur, November 9-15, 1995, p. 96). As if there were a contradiction between the vital affirmation and suicide, and as if the latter were precisely, given the circumstances, the only way to further support this life. Now, Deleuze had no need to be "saved" by the last phrase of D. Eribon's article, who, forgetting with magnanimity "this frightening image of the philosopher vanquished by the weakness of the body and throwing himself from the window to escape illness" (which he himself created from these pieces), concedes, great lord, that it is better for him to try and see, while remaining thus, a final laughter of the heretic.</p>

<p>The sense of this suicide or of this "unimaginable image" (as Derrida says) can also be approached through a distinction of conceptual types put in place by Deleuze in Difference and Repetition: that of "empirical exercise" and that of "transcendental exercise." The empirical exercise is the normal course of experience, with its settings and landmarks, its "recognitions"; to think, to act, in this busy banality of business and conformity to oneself, is simply to recognize the established patterns of thought and action; otherwise, to do nothing and say nothing, to let the quiet music slide through our common places. From this empirical order, in exchange for some heart-wrenching words "it is a finger, it is a table, hello Théétète" (DR, p. 181). But it happens that, under certain conditions, the empirical exercise fails, that it meets a limit, that it bumps into its own impotence, that all the arsenal of its "already-said" is no longer of any use in the face of the arrival of something truly extraordinary. For example, for Descartes, the Malicious Genius erupts into "natural reason"—that is to say, in the empirical exercise, which was established for each of us since the most tender childhood, through the immemorial chatter of our nurses. Faced with this evidence, Descartes engages himself in a "transcendental exercise" of thought, in a thought of crisis, hyperbolic doubt, and invents a new type of evidence to measure this unheard-of enemy that is the Malicious Genius, namely the impregnable and diamond-like solidity of the Cogito. In short, faced with the dead end, with the impossible, of two choices one is: either one lets oneself sink, a line of mortifying abolition, drowned in the chaos; or one reacts and passes to "transcendental exercise," which means an invention beyond the impossible, where one abandons old criteria and stereotypes, and opens new possibilities of these bourgeois liberties built entirely on this first theft, on this false hypothesis and on this false liberty, from which the surplus-value is derived.</p>

<p>How to begin to conceive this imperative character of life toward its affirmation, and the fact that some, however, give their lives to death? It is necessary to understand that death is the pursuit of life by other means, the passage to a "transcendental exercise" of life, from circumstances where its "empirical exercise" is no longer possible. As the rarefaction of oxygen, as asphyxia which had rendered life impossible, so that the only way to continue to affirm it was the invention of flight, transcendental exercise. Sartre, in Saint Genet Comedian and Martyr, describes similar situations of impossibility or impotence of results, where every outlet is blocked, yet voluntary liberty finds one more way to affirm itself, it holds onto two examples: some, victims of chance, the victims of an airplane accident, inaccessible to all their rescues, "torn apart, burned," condemned to die from horrible sufferings; some, prey to the inhumanity of other men, a young Jewish adolescent during the last war, "imprisoned" and knowing himself condemned and waiting for his execution as a hostage (p. 384 SARTRE, Saint Genet comedian and martyr, Gallimard, Paris, 1985, cited SG.).</p>

<p>In the two cases, there is nothing to do, death is certain. And in the two cases, liberty cannot avoid not passing through one last resource, a final response, which could be characterized, in Sartrean terms, as one last affirmation of the cogito: I who am currently being inexorably annihilated by the blind force of things, or by the malevolent will of men, I still remain always available to my own disposition this minimal reproduction of myself which consists in the lucidity that it is I who am occupied in disappearing; I, my despair, my convulsions, my "moral tetanus," but I refuse and yet cry out to the face of Nothingness: the imaginary, says Sartre, is that which remains of action when action is impossible; if so remains the life when death is inexorable. Death never has reason of me, since although I die, I am still compelled to resist death, so I must live, and when I am dead, in truth there is no more 'I' that could be affected by any defeat. "There is no more" plus the death, impersonal redistribution of materiality, "inferior splashing whatever of the real."</p>

<p>In Deleuzian terms, he says: "counter-effect." To speak of that which arrives, and which is accidental, not by wanting it, but "by the flash, of the splendor of the event," that is to say, from "sense" (LS, p. 175). This counter-effect through which the event arrives, from which few things separate. Sartre was everything to me. Sartre was something phenomenal, said Deleuze in an interview with D. Eribon, parts of which were published in Le Nouvel Observateur (November 16-22, 1995, p. 114). This admiration, which contradicts a common critique—motivated by the "dialectical" range that Sartre grants to nothingness and to negation—was already known to him as his expected ends: it is the Transcendence of the Ego to which he once paid homage, seeing, in the impersonal transcendental field advocated by Sartre, from which he makes a non-conscious of the ego, the sketch of that which he will radicalize and call "singularities" pre-individual. But, in the light of this death, as well as this possible interpretation in terms of passage to a transcendental exercise of life, one can venture a supplementary hypothesis: it is, in short, the general breath of liberty, not only the conceptual technicality particular to the Transcendence of the Ego, but a certain conception of vital life that joins Deleuze and Sartre.</p>

<p>One can apply the distinction between empirical exercise and transcendental exercise to life itself. In general, the reproduction of the course of life is, for itself, either empirical or natural, peaceful and redundant adaptation of a body to its environment. This harmony can go as far as masking, by not showing it, the violently imperative character that vital reproduction of the organism takes on. This imperative, which Sartre has in common with Deleuze through a shared resolutely vitalist inspiration, expresses itself as follows: "The possibility of being to life is not given with life itself, but the reality resides in the perpetual continuation of his being [...] In the midst of organic life as a possible absolute of itself, the only one of which is praxis is the indefinite reproduction of life" (CRD, p. 300). Life cannot not reproduce: therefore, notably, the servitude inherent to the so-called "free" labor contract, which rests on the fiction of a sovereign choice hypothesis with regard to the worker, if you want to earn a salary... alas, sell me your time and your force, and you become a slave. The worker cannot not want a salary, because he must reproduce his life, since he is constrained by a system of exploitation, and that the regime perhaps of the "Sartrean assumption," according to which only counts what we make of what has been made of us. Sartre and Deleuze meet without doubt at this enigmatic point where "Amor fati does nothing but fight with the combat of free men" (id.), at this point where one must play even if the games are played, live still although one is already dead. Thus makes sense the imaginary flight of the exhausted deprived of all possibility.</p>

